      "heading": "The loop is the agent",
      "narration": "Everything so far has been one-shot: you send a prompt, you get text, the interaction is over. RAG extended that by putting more information into the prompt, but the shape was unchanged — one call, one answer. An agent is a different shape, and the difference is a loop. Instead of answering immediately, the model is given a set of actions it may take, and at each step it either produces a final answer or asks for an action to be performed. Your code performs it, feeds the result back, and asks again. The model can now find out things it did not know when it started, and change what it does based on what it learns. Three ingredients, and if any one is missing you do not have an agent. First, tools: a defined set of things the model is allowed to request. Second, observations: after each tool runs, its result goes back into the conversation, so the model's next decision is informed by what actually happened rather than by what it guessed would happen. Third, a termination condition: some way for the loop to end, either because the model says it is finished or because you cut it off. That third one sounds administrative and it is the one people get wrong. Now the most important architectural fact in this lesson, and it is a security fact as much as a design one. The model does not execute anything. It cannot. All it can do is emit text saying: I would like to call the function named search-database with these arguments. That is a request, it arrives in your code, and your code decides whether to honour it. Nothing happens that your program did not choose to do. This means every guardrail you will ever want — validating arguments, requiring approval for destructive operations, rate limiting, checking permissions, refusing calls outright — lives at that one boundary in your own code, not in the prompt. Prompts are advisory. The dispatch function is enforcement. Which leads to the framing for the whole lesson: the intelligence is the model's, and the control flow is yours. The model is very good at deciding what to do next given what it has seen. It is not in charge of anything. An agent framework, underneath its abstractions, is a while-loop with good error handling and a schema validator — and that is genuinely all it is, which is why you should write one yourself, in about sixty lines, before you adopt somebody else's. Once you have, every framework becomes readable, because you know what it is wrapping.",
