      "heading": "Tools: the description is the interface",
      "narration": "A tool is less exciting than it sounds. It is an ordinary function in your codebase — search the database, fetch a URL, run a calculation, send an email — plus a schema describing it: a name, a description in plain language, and a typed list of parameters. You send those schemas along with your request, and the model, instead of writing prose about what it would like to happen, returns a structured call: this function, these arguments, as JSON. That structure is the whole reason modern tool use works reliably. In the early days people asked the model to write out a command and then parsed it with regular expressions, and it was miserable, because the model would helpfully vary the format. Now the provider constrains the output to your schema, and you get something you can dispatch on directly. Now the sentence I most want you to keep from this lesson: the description is the API contract, and it is written for the model, not for a human reading your source. When the model is deciding whether to use a tool, the description is all it has. Not the implementation, not the tests, not the docstring conventions of your team. Those few sentences. So write them for that purpose: say what the tool does, say when to use it, say explicitly when not to use it, and say what it returns and in what shape. Include an example argument if the format is at all unusual. A description like queries the database is technically true and useless, because it does not tell the model which of your three database tools to reach for. The practical consequence is a debugging rule that saves a lot of time. When an agent keeps choosing the wrong tool, the instinct is to add instructions to the system prompt telling it not to. That works badly. The problem is almost always that two tool descriptions do not draw a clear boundary between themselves, and the fix is to rewrite those two descriptions so that the boundary is explicit — one says use this for current inventory, the other says use this for historical sales, and the ambiguity is gone. Tool selection bugs are documentation bugs, and treating them that way is much faster than prompt engineering around them. Two design rules to close. Keep the tool set small and orthogonal. Five tools that clearly do different things will be used correctly; twenty with overlapping purposes will not, because you have made a decision problem harder for no gain, and you are also paying tokens for every schema on every call. And make every tool return its errors as readable text rather than raising into your loop. If a search finds nothing, return no results found for that query rather than throwing. If an argument was invalid, return a message saying which one and why. The model can read that and try something else, which is the entire point of the loop — an exception, by contrast, just ends the run.",
