      "heading": "ReAct, written out",
      "narration": "Here is the loop in full, and it is short enough to hold in your head. Start with the user's request in a message list. Send that list, along with your tool schemas, to the model. The response is one of two things: a final answer, in which case you return it and stop; or a request to call a tool. If it is a tool call, look up the function, validate the arguments, run it, and append the result to the message list as an observation. Then send the whole list again. Repeat until you get a final answer or until you hit a step limit. That is it — perhaps sixty lines with error handling, and every agent framework in existence is a decorated version of it. ReAct, which stands for reasoning and acting, adds one small thing to that loop and it is worth doing. Before the model chooses an action, ask it to state its reasoning: what it is trying to establish and why this tool is the right next step. So each iteration produces a thought, then an action, then an observation. This has two benefits and both are real. The measured one is that making the reasoning explicit improves the quality of the tool choice — the same effect as chain-of-thought prompting, applied to a decision instead of an arithmetic problem. The practical one is that you get a readable trace. When an agent does something baffling, the thought line tells you what it believed at that moment, and you can usually see exactly where the reasoning went wrong. Debugging an agent without that trace is very unpleasant. Now the property that surprises people about cost. Every iteration sends the entire history — the original request, every thought, every tool call, every observation. So the tenth step is paying for everything that happened in the first nine. Cost grows with the square of the number of steps, not linearly, and a twenty-step agent run can cost far more than you estimated from a single call. The mitigation is not simply a bigger context window; it is to summarize older steps once the history gets long, or to keep results in an external scratchpad and put only a reference in the conversation. And finally the guardrail that is not optional: cap the number of steps. An agent that has misunderstood its task will not notice; it will keep calling tools, forever, spending money on every call, and the loop has no natural reason to end. A maximum of ten or fifteen iterations, with a clear message when it is hit, is one line of code. Leaving it out is the single most common way an agent reaches production with a hole in it, and the way you find out is usually a bill.",
