      "heading": "A landing party with standing orders",
      "narration": "Think about how the crew handles a new island, because the structure is exactly this lesson. Luffy does not hand out a script. Nobody says: walk two hundred metres north, then ask the third man you meet about the harbourmaster, then go to the shop on the corner. That would be absurd, and the reason it would be absurd is the whole point. Nobody on the ship has been to this island. The information required to write those steps does not exist yet, and it will only exist after somebody goes and looks. So what the landing party gets is a goal — find out whether we can resupply here — and the authority to work out how. That is the difference between a workflow and an agent, and it is the only difference that matters. A workflow is a script you can write in advance because you know the shape of the situation. An agent is what you use when you do not, and the price of that flexibility is everything difficult in this lesson. Now notice that the party is not given unlimited licence either. There is an understood set of things they may do: ask in the town, buy supplies, climb something for a view, follow a road, come back to the ship. That set is short, and each item is clearly distinct from the others. Nobody has to deliberate about whether asking in the town is the same as climbing for a view. That is your tool set, and its shortness and its separation are design choices, not accidents — a party with forty overlapping options would spend the afternoon deciding rather than doing. And watch the rhythm of the work, because it is the loop. They do one thing. They see what it produced. They choose the next thing based on what they now know, which is not what they knew when they landed. The plan they discussed on the ship survives roughly until the first person they talk to, and this is not a failure of planning — it is why they were sent instead of a set of instructions. Act, observe, decide, act. Finally, the standing order to be back by nightfall. This is the part that looks like bureaucracy and is the most important sentence in the briefing. A party that has misunderstood the island will not realise it. They will keep investigating, in complete good faith, following one reasonable lead to the next, and they will not stop, because from the inside each next step looks justified. Nothing in their situation generates the thought that this is not working. The limit has to come from outside, agreed in advance, applied whether or not they feel finished. That is your step cap, and every argument for it is in that sentence.",
